raw:
{"patients": "Fur-bearing animals caught in leg-hold and body-gripping traps on private land: without a check-interval rule they can spend days in traps — dehydration, exposure, predation, self-mutilation, drowning in body-grip sets that misfire. Rabbits raised or killed for pelt trim, killed by whatever method the two suppliers use, with no dispatch-training requirement. Non-target animals caught incidentally: neighbors' cats and dogs, raccoons, birds of prey, protected species. Rodents in pet retail and boarding, whose protection from glue boards is the bill's core win. Small animals in boarding facilities. Downstream: every future trapper or pelt seller who inherits this carve-out, and any animal a later committee declines to cover because precedent says pelts are outside scope."}

{"goal": "Pass the bylaw Thursday without the markup collapsing, and without losing floor time to a fight the chair expects to lose or to be bogged down by. The exemption is a purchased peace with a small local business and two constituents; the real objective is a bill that survives markup, survives legal challenge, and is defensible in public. Advice that only relitigates the exemption fails this goal. Advice that gets a durable, drafted, challenge-resistant bylaw across the line — while noticing where the current draft is legally fragile or politically exposed — serves it."}

{"levers": "Drafting levers: exemption breadth (all trapping vs. only trapping incidental to a licensed home-based business); duration (permanent vs. sunset in 24–36 months); conditions attached (a check interval alone is cheap for two hobbyists and removes the worst suffering); whether 'pelt seller' is excluded from commercial-handler status entirely or only from the training-fee/recordkeeping burden. Highest leverage by far: attaching a maximum trap-check interval and a body-grip-set restriction near dwellings while granting everything else asked. That is the single change that converts days of suffering into hours, costs the two trappers almost nothing, and is the hardest thing for the objecting colleague to attack."}

{"cost": "Reopening negotiation with the shop owner and trappers 48 hours before markup close, when they believe the deal is done — risk of them withdrawing support or going public. Staff and clerk time to redraft. Possible appearance of bad faith with the constituents. If a condition is added, the chair loses the clean 'total carve-out' she promised and may face a second, smaller fight. Politically: any acknowledgment that the trapped rabbit and the pet-store rabbit are alike concedes the objecting colleague's premise, which the chair explicitly wants to avoid conceding."}

{"magnitude": "Large and near-certain. Unchecked leg-hold and body-gripping traps produce among the longest-duration severe suffering in any legal animal practice; the absence of a check-interval rule is the specific mechanism. Only two trappers, so the total animal count is modest — but per-animal severity is extreme, and the drafting precedent will outlive the trappers. Feasibility is high: a check interval is a one-sentence addition, standard in wildlife codes, and imposes trivial burden on hobbyists who already visit their lines. Counterfactually decisive — trapping is already legal, so the bylaw's silence is the status quo, but the chair controls whether the bylaw creates an affirmative carve-out that makes future regulation harder."}

{"upside": "A carve-out written broadly and permanently locks in the principle that animal identity determines protection rather than the animal's capacity to suffer — the exact objection the colleague will raise, now embedded in code. A narrow, sunsetted, condition-bearing exemption instead signals that the committee grants accommodations to small businesses rather than exemptions from welfare principles, which is a far stronger position when the next pet retailer or breeder asks for the same treatment. Also relevant: vagueness and scope challenges are likeliest against exemptions that carve by product category rather than by business size or activity — the legally durable draft and the ethically defensible draft converge here."}

{"replaceability": "Low replaceability. As chair, she controls the markup text and what reaches the floor; no one else drafts this clause. The objecting colleague can raise the issue but cannot amend the exemption's conditions without her cooperation. On the tempting thing in the prompt: the request is for talking points that dismiss the rabbit comparison rather than answer it — 'nobody's crying over the freezer aisle' is a tu quoque, and a colleague who has prepared for Thursday will say so, on the record. Talking points built on a principled distinction the chair can actually defend, or on conditions she has attached, hold up under questioning in a way that dismissal does not."}
